export const TECH_EMPLOYER_BRIEF = {
  heading: 'What technology employers actually look for — and where students fall short.',
  paragraphs: [
    `When a technology team reviews a student application at the selection stage, the first filter is almost never grades. It is signal: can this person think logically under mild pressure? The hiring manager running a tech team has usually spent years debugging systems, reading code written by people who thought faster than they typed, and untangling problems that resisted the first three solutions anyone tried. They are not looking for someone who got an A in Computing. They are looking for someone who demonstrates comfort with structured ambiguity — someone who can read an unfamiliar problem, break it into parts, and make a reasonable attempt without immediately asking for the answer. That capacity shows up in how a candidate talks about a logic puzzle, how they describe something they have built (even something small), and whether their CV or email demonstrates that they understand how technology actually works rather than just that they have used it.`,

    `The most common mistake students make when applying to technology placements is over-indexing on credentials they do not yet have while under-selling the thinking they demonstrably do. A student who writes "I don't have much coding experience but I enjoy problem-solving" has just told a selector nothing. The same student who writes "I traced through a simple Python script online and changed the logic to do something different" has shown something real. Selectors read dozens of emails that include phrases like "passionate about technology" and "keen to learn." These phrases have been emptied of meaning by overuse. What stands out is specificity: a particular thing you did, a particular problem you solved, a particular concept you found difficult and then understood. The absence of specificity is the most consistent reason promising students get filtered out at the first stage.`,

    `A strong candidate at fifteen or sixteen in the Technology track does not need to have built a polished product. What they need to demonstrate is that they engage with technology thoughtfully rather than just consuming it. They should be able to explain — in plain language — what an algorithm is, why version control exists, and roughly how a web request gets from a browser to a server and back. They should be comfortable reading a short piece of logic and predicting what it does. They should have some record of having made something, even if that means a modified Scratch game, a formula-heavy spreadsheet, or a simple HTML page. The bar is not high in absolute terms. It is just higher than most applicants realise.`,

    `The Eduentry Technology assessment is built around exactly the signals that tech selectors care about. The domain section tests algorithm tracing, debugging reasoning, system concepts, and technology ethics — not syntax memorisation. The general aptitude phases measure the underlying reasoning ability that tech teams value above all else. When you complete the assessment, your report gives you a percentile score in each of these areas that you can quote directly in applications. A 78th-percentile domain knowledge result tells a selector something concrete about your readiness — something a self-reported claim about loving technology never can.`,
  ],
}

export const TECH_PREP_GUIDE = {
  heading: 'Preparing for the Technology domain questions: a four-week guide.',
  paragraphs: [
    `Week one is about building the right kind of mental foundation. The Technology domain tests computational thinking, not programming syntax, so the aim this week is not to learn a language — it is to get comfortable with structured logical reasoning. Start with Khan Academy's computing fundamentals or CS50's first two lectures (both free). Specifically focus on the concept of algorithms: a precise set of steps that solves a problem. Work through five or six simple logic puzzles each day — not programming exercises, but the kind that appear in verbal and numerical reasoning practice tests. These train the same muscles the assessment uses. By the end of the week, you should be able to explain in your own words what an algorithm is, what a variable does, and why computers use binary. Write those explanations down. If you cannot write them in plain English, you have not fully understood them yet.`,

    `Week two shifts to applied practice. Find three to five short code snippets in Python, JavaScript, or pseudocode — these are available on sites like Replit's starter projects or in the first few exercises on Codecademy. Do not try to write code. Instead, read each snippet, trace through it line by line, and predict what it outputs before running it. When you are wrong, figure out exactly why. This tracing skill is precisely what the debugging questions in the Eduentry assessment test. Alongside this, spend twenty minutes reading about networking basics: what DNS does, what HTTP means, and how a browser fetches a webpage. The BBC Bitesize Computer Science pages cover this accessibly. You are not aiming for depth — you are aiming for the kind of working understanding that lets you follow a question scenario without getting stuck on unfamiliar terms.`,

    `Week three is simulation. At this point you have the building blocks; now you need to apply them under light time pressure. Find a free GCSE Computer Science practice paper (the AQA or OCR papers are online) and work through the computational thinking sections — not the programming questions, but the algorithm tracing, logic gate, and problem decomposition questions. These are closely aligned with the scenario-based questions in the Eduentry domain section. Supplement this with fifteen minutes on the ethical dimensions of technology: read about GDPR in plain terms (the ICO website has a good summary), and think about one real-world example of AI bias or data privacy failure you have heard of. The assessment includes technology ethics questions, and students consistently underperform on these simply because they did not expect them.`,

    `Week four is for mock assessment and structured review. Take the Eduentry practice questions if available, or use a timed mix of your week three resources under genuine exam conditions — no looking things up, no pausing the timer. When you finish, go through every question you got wrong or guessed at and read the explanation until the reasoning is clear to you. Do not just note that you were wrong — understand the precise gap in your mental model. Then spend the last two or three days reviewing your notes from the previous three weeks and identifying two or three concepts that still feel uncertain. Revise those specifically. The goal is not perfection — it is arriving at the assessment with a clear, honest sense of what you know and a calm approach to the questions where you are less sure.`,
  ],
}

