export const BUSINESS_EMPLOYER_BRIEF = {
  heading: 'What business employers actually look for — and where students fall short.',
  paragraphs: [
    `At the selection stage for a business internship, the quality that most reliably separates shortlisted candidates from the rest is commercial awareness — and it is not the same thing as knowing business vocabulary. Selectors at banks, consultancies, and commercial teams are not testing whether a student can recite the definition of EBITDA. They are testing whether the student pays genuine attention to the commercial world: whether they read the news with curiosity about why companies make the decisions they do, whether they can take a business scenario and reason through it without defaulting to the first obvious answer, and whether they understand that business problems are almost always trade-offs rather than clean solutions. A student who has read one week's worth of Financial Times with a pen in hand and thought carefully about what they read will outperform a student who has memorised a textbook glossary every time.`,

    `The most consistent mistake business applicants make is being generic at every stage of the application process. "I am interested in finance" is not a sentence that gets anyone shortlisted. "I read about the Tesco / Booker merger and tried to work out why the synergies were harder to capture than analysts expected" is. Selectors at competitive business internship programmes — spring insight weeks, FTSE 100 schemes, consulting junior programmes — are experienced readers. They know what a prepared candidate looks like and what an unprepared one sounds like. The former has specific examples. The latter has enthusiasm without evidence. The gap between them is rarely talent — it is usually that the prepared candidate spent three weeks reading and thinking and the unprepared one spent three weeks intending to.`,

    `A strong candidate at fifteen or sixteen in the Business track has two qualities that are both rarer and more valuable than good grades. The first is genuine curiosity about how commercial decisions get made: they have opinions about why certain businesses succeed and others fail, they can describe a recent business story in their own words, and they notice the economics of everyday transactions. The second quality is structured thinking: when faced with a business problem or a case study scenario, they do not jump to an answer — they identify the key variables, consider what information is missing, and reason towards a recommendation that acknowledges trade-offs. Neither of these qualities requires any formal business education. They are the product of curiosity and deliberate practice.`,

    `The Eduentry Business assessment is aligned to exactly what commercial selectors measure at the first stage. The domain section tests market reasoning, financial literacy at a conceptual level, business case analysis, and stakeholder thinking — all scenario-based, none rote recall. The verbal reasoning section tests exactly the structured thinking that business roles require. A strong Eduentry result in the Business track gives you a verifiable, third-party signal that you are ready for a commercial environment — which is a significantly more credible signal than any personal statement claim about your passion for business.`,
  ],
}

export const BUSINESS_PREP_GUIDE = {
  heading: 'Preparing for the Business domain questions: a four-week guide.',
  paragraphs: [
    `Week one is about building commercial context. The single most effective thing you can do is read the BBC Business section and one quality newspaper's business pages every day for a week — not to memorise facts, but to notice patterns: how companies talk about their results, what drives price changes, when a market story reveals something about competitive strategy. Alongside this, spend thirty minutes each day working through the core concepts of a P&L statement: what revenue, gross profit, operating costs, and net profit mean, and what it tells you when one of them moves. You do not need an accounting textbook. A good five-minute YouTube explainer plus working through one or two simple examples yourself will be enough. By the end of the week, you should be able to read a simple income statement and identify the most important line for a given business question.`,

    `Week two is applied commercial reasoning. Find three recent business case studies — these are freely available on the BBC, Financial Times Student, or via the Business Case Studies website — and for each one, write a short paragraph answering: what was the business problem, what decision was made, and was it the right one? Focus on your reasoning, not your conclusion. Then move to SWOT analysis practice: take any company you know well — a retailer, a streaming service, a food chain — and complete a SWOT grid for them from memory. The point is not to get it "right" — there is no right answer. The point is to practise structured commercial thinking under mild pressure, which is what the Eduentry domain section tests.`,

    `Week three is about simulation under time pressure. Download a past paper for GCSE Business Studies or Economics (free from AQA and Edexcel) and work through the extended answer questions — specifically the "recommend and justify" questions where you are asked to choose between two options and explain your reasoning. These questions are structured almost identically to several of the Eduentry business domain scenarios. Give yourself twenty minutes per question, write your answer without looking anything up, and then review it against the mark scheme. You are training yourself to structure commercial arguments under time pressure — a skill that will serve you in both the assessment and any subsequent interview.`,

    `Week four is mock assessment and gap-filling. Set up a practice session that mirrors the actual Eduentry assessment: timed, no notes, no looking things up. If you have access to Eduentry practice questions, use those. If not, use a commercial awareness question bank (many are available free online for bank and consulting spring schemes). Review every question you missed or were uncertain about. Then spend the last two or three days on the areas where you felt weakest — most commonly this is financial literacy (understanding margin versus profit) or business case reasoning (how to weigh competing priorities). A short, focused review of those specific concepts in week four will have more impact than starting a new topic.`,
  ],
}

