export const DATA_EMPLOYER_BRIEF = {
  heading: 'What data analytics employers actually look for — and where students fall short.',
  paragraphs: [
    `Data analytics selectors are filtering for a specific capacity that many students fail to demonstrate even when they genuinely have it: the ability to move from raw numbers to a meaningful finding without being told what the finding should be. When a data team reviews a student application, the question they are implicitly asking is: if we put this person in front of a dataset and asked them what was interesting, would they find something real? That ability does not require a degree in statistics. It requires careful, curious attention to what numbers actually say — not what they could be made to say, but what they genuinely show. Students who approach data with a predetermined conclusion, who reach for the most obvious interpretation, or who mistake noise for signal will struggle at placement level. Students who approach data with patient, honest curiosity almost always thrive.`,

    `The most common mistake data applicants make is conflating familiarity with tools with actual data literacy. A student who says "I know Excel and I've used Google Sheets" has described tool access, not skill. A student who says "I noticed that the averages in two columns of my school project data were misleading because of three outliers, so I reported the median instead" has demonstrated something real. Data teams are full of people who know their tools. What they are short of — and what they are willing to pay attention to in a sixteen-year-old student — is the kind of careful, questioning approach to numbers that produces reliable insights rather than impressive-looking but unreliable conclusions. That quality shows up in language, in examples, and in how a candidate explains their reasoning about a data problem.`,

    `A strong candidate at fifteen or sixteen in the Data Analytics track has typically done at least one of the following: used a spreadsheet to answer a question they genuinely wanted to answer (not just for a school project), read and interpreted a graph or data story in a news article and thought critically about what it did and did not show, or played with a public dataset (the ONS, Kaggle, or a sports statistics site) out of personal curiosity. None of these require formal training. What they share is a habit of treating data as something that tells a story rather than just a set of numbers to be processed. That habit, combined with comfort with basic statistical concepts — mean, median, distribution, correlation — is the foundation of every strong data analyst.`,

    `The Eduentry Data Analytics assessment tests exactly the skills that data selectors look for at entry level. The domain section covers chart interpretation, descriptive statistics, data quality identification, spreadsheet reasoning, SQL reading, and data ethics — all scenario-based, all at a level accessible to a well-prepared student without any formal data science training. A strong result in the Data Analytics track gives you a verified, percentile-ranked signal of your data literacy that you can reference in every application you make to data teams — including, critically, in responses to the inevitable question about why you are interested in data when you are still at school.`,
  ],
}

export const DATA_PREP_GUIDE = {
  heading: 'Preparing for the Data Analytics domain questions: a four-week guide.',
  paragraphs: [
    `Week one is about developing the habit of reading data critically. Every day, find one chart or graph in the news — BBC News, The Guardian, the ONS website, and Statista all publish accessible charts — and spend five minutes genuinely interrogating it: what does it show, what does it not show, is the scale misleading, what question would you ask before drawing a conclusion from it? This habit is the single most effective thing you can do to prepare for the chart and graph reading questions in the domain section. Alongside this, revisit (or encounter for the first time) the core descriptive statistics concepts: mean, median, mode, range, and what outliers do to each of them. Khan Academy's statistics unit covers this clearly and for free. Focus on understanding when each measure is useful and when it is misleading — that judgement is what the assessment tests.`,

    `Week two is applied practice with real data. Download a free, simple dataset from the ONS or from Google's public dataset tools, open it in Google Sheets or Excel, and spend thirty minutes answering three questions you choose yourself: which category is largest, which is shrinking fastest, what is the average, is the average representative? Then practise the spreadsheet reasoning questions that the domain section tests: use IF, VLOOKUP, and SUMIF on a small practice sheet, not to become an expert but to understand what logic each formula applies. Sites like W3Schools and the Google Sheets help documentation explain these clearly. The goal is to be able to look at a formula in a question scenario and trace through its logic rather than being stopped by unfamiliar syntax.`,

    `Week three is SQL and ethics. The data analytics domain section includes SQL reading questions — you will be shown a SELECT query with a WHERE clause, a GROUP BY, or a JOIN, and asked what data it returns. You do not need to write SQL. You need to be able to read it. Spend two sessions of thirty minutes on this using the free SQLZoo interactive tutorials, focusing specifically on the SELECT and WHERE sections. Then turn to data ethics: read the ICO's plain-language summary of GDPR, and look up one real example of a data privacy breach or an algorithmic bias case. The ethics questions in the domain section are straightforward once you have the frameworks — consent, anonymisation, fairness — but students consistently neglect this area in preparation.`,

    `Week four is mock assessment and structured review. Take the Eduentry practice questions in timed conditions, or use a data literacy quiz (several are available from NHS Digital's data literacy resources and from the Open Data Institute). Review every question you missed — not just to note the correct answer but to understand exactly which concept you misapplied. The most common failure modes are: confusing median and mean in interpretation questions, misreading chart scales (especially dual-axis charts), and choosing correlation as a causal explanation. If any of these appear in your review, spend a focused session on each one before the real assessment. Arrive at the assessment having practised the habit of reading slowly and carefully — data questions almost always reward patience over speed.`,
  ],
}

