export const DIGITAL_PREP_GUIDE = {
  heading: 'Preparing for the Digital Marketing domain questions: a four-week guide.',
  paragraphs: [
    `Week one is about building a working vocabulary for digital marketing metrics. The single most common source of failure in the domain section is not understanding the relationship between the key metrics: impressions, reach, click-through rate, conversion rate, cost-per-click, and return on ad spend. These are not complicated concepts, but they are precise ones, and confusing them in a scenario question will cost you marks. Spend thirty minutes each day this week learning each metric: what it measures, what it does not measure, and what a high or low value typically means. Google's own Digital Garage course (free, beginner-friendly) covers most of these well. Supplement this with one daily exercise: when you see an advert online, think about what metric its creator is most likely optimising for and why. That habit of reading marketing with a professional eye will serve you throughout the preparation period.`,

    `Week two is SEO and content strategy. The domain section tests SEO at a conceptual level — you do not need to have run an SEO campaign, but you do need to understand why certain on-page factors affect search rankings and why others do not. Spend two sessions this week reading Moz's Beginner's Guide to SEO (free online) — specifically the sections on keyword intent and on-page optimisation. Then practise content strategy reasoning by doing the following exercise each day: take a real piece of content (a website page, a social post, an email you received) and assess it against a brief: who is the audience, what action do they want the audience to take, and is the content designed to produce that action? This exercise trains the kind of critical assessment the content marketing questions in the domain section require.`,

    `Week three is analytics and campaign planning. The domain section includes questions about reading a Google Analytics-style dashboard and making a decision based on what the data shows. Spend one session this week creating a free Google Analytics demo account (Google provides one pre-loaded with data from the Google Merchandise Store) and exploring what each section shows. Focus specifically on: the difference between sessions and users, what bounce rate means and what it does not, and how to identify which channel is driving the most valuable traffic. Separately, practise campaign planning by choosing a hypothetical product launch and mapping out the channel sequence: what goes first, what follows, how budget might be allocated, and what success metrics you would use. This kind of structured planning question appears in several forms in the domain section.`,

    `Week four is mock assessment and compliance review. Complete a timed practice session covering all four domain areas — SEO, social strategy, analytics, and paid media. Review every question you missed and identify whether the gap was conceptual (you did not understand the principle) or interpretive (you understood the principle but misread the scenario). Conceptual gaps should be addressed with targeted revision of the relevant area. Interpretive gaps are usually solved by slowing down and reading scenario questions more carefully. Spend the last two days on digital marketing compliance: read a plain-language summary of GDPR as it applies to marketing (the direct marketing rules on the ICO website), look up the ASA's rules on influencer disclosure, and read one real example of a brand being fined for a compliance failure. These ethics and compliance questions appear in the domain section and are consistently underprepared for.`,
  ],
}

